From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: REPLACE-ME <you@example>
Date: Sun, 04 Oct 2026 08:48:28 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] avfilter: add hdr10plus dynamic-metadata producer filter

Computes the per-picture ST 2094-40 statistics (maxscl, average_maxrgb and the
nine distribution_maxrgb percentiles) and attaches them as
AV_FRAME_DATA_DYNAMIC_HDR_PLUS, so encoder wrappers that already write that
side data carry HDR10+ in-band unchanged: measured here on libaom-av1, 12/12
pictures come back through a decoder with the values the filter produced
(libsvtav1 needs the accompanying patch; its T35 channel was verified against
the SVT-AV1 library directly). Serialisation is left to
av_dynamic_hdr_plus_to_t35(); the filter never touches a bitstream.

Granularity note for reviewers, because it is a property of the carrier and not of this
filter: writing a metadata OBU is not the same as a consumer receiving one. Measured on
this box, a libaom encode of a 12-picture HDR10+ source delivers 12/12 pictures with the
metadata intact, while an unrelated per-picture T35 writer in a hardware wrapper
delivered 7/12 - the pictures that lost it are those sharing a temporal unit with other
picture OBUs. So per-picture statistics survive a one-picture-per-temporal-unit encode,
and anything coarser should be checked decoder-side rather than assumed.

The statistics are normalised by the 10000 cd/m2 PQ full scale, as 4.4 requires, so
no reference peak enters them. TargetedSystemDisplayMaximumLuminance describes the
display the metadata is aimed at and defaults to the 1000 cd/m2 reference display;
peak= overrides it.

For ApplicationVersion = 1 the nine percentile positions, the two reserved fixed
entries and a single window are all mandated (8.5.4, 9.4), so none of that is
offered as a knob: the only option is peak.

Input whose transfer is not ST 2084, and studio-range RGB, are refused: the
statistics are only meaningful on PQ code values, and a wrong-domain frame still
serialises and decodes cleanly, so nothing downstream could tell the difference.

Metadata already on the frame is replaced rather than added
alongside it. An HEVC decoder attaches an authored HDR10+ block as
AV_FRAME_DATA_DYNAMIC_HDR_PLUS, that entry survives the format
conversions a normal graph does, and av_frame_new_side_data() appends
- so a second entry would leave the authored one first, and every
first-match reader, including the encoder wrappers that carry this
type, would publish the input's numbers while the ones measured here
went nowhere. Measured on a studio-authored 4K clip: before this
replace, the plain, curve=rolloff, peak=4000 and even the no-producer
graphs each produced the same 68,679-byte AV1 file, byte for byte.
An input that carries nothing is unaffected: with nothing to replace,
the encode is the same bytes the pre-change build made on the same
input.

Two depths are accepted, 10 and 12 bit planar RGB, because ST 2084's input is the
NORMALISED code: a 12-bit full-scale sample is 4095/4095, and a filter that divides
by 1023 regardless reports a 12-bit white as a quarter of one. Nothing else in the
measurement changes with depth, so depth is carried as a runtime value - the code
width comes from the pixel format descriptor, the distribution table is sized from
it, and the sample mask is the same width instead of a constant 0x3FF. A frame whose
depth is neither 10 nor 12 is refused rather than measured through a table that is
too small for it: at depth 0 the mask would be `& 0`, every pixel would bin at code 0,
and the payload would still serialise as a confident all-black picture.

New filter plus its registration in one commit, so the tree builds at every
step of the series.

Signed-off-by: REPLACE-ME <you@example>
---

The tree this diff was generated against: ffmpeg (HEAD 8864fd0)
Verification: compiles warning-free as one TU (cl -W3, MSVC 19.51) against
this revision, control arm upstream's own vf_setparams.c, both at 0 warnings;
filter_frame() executed on
41 picture cases spanning BOTH accepted depths (179 assertions, the first of which
rebuilds this file's bytes from the patch's own '+' lines and requires them to match
what gets compiled); the code->coded mapping was walked exhaustively over all 1024
10-bit and all 4096 12-bit codes against a reference implementation, every statistic
field matched, and the serialised T35 body matched byte for byte. The filter was then
built into a whole tree and run inside a real filtergraph: 25 assertions / 0 FAIL,
whose first arms pin that the binary under test was compiled from these exact sources,
and which were re-run after the side-data change below (upstream-kit/README.md).

The second depth was also measured on a genuinely 12-bit source rather than on a
converted one: a 2048x858 12-bit-per-component JPEG 2000 X'Y'Z' cinema master (the
CC-BY-3.0 Sintel 2K DCP) decodes to xyz12le and reaches this filter as gbrp12le with
12-bit codes actually in use - max 3270 of 4095, 75.1% of samples carrying information
below the 10-bit boundary - and 24 of its pictures come out as 24 blocks that two
builds other than the one that wrote them read back with identical values. Feeding the
same 24 pictures at 10 bit instead moves all 24 payloads, which is what shows the
depth is read rather than assumed. That graph's colour labels are asserted by the
caller to keep the 12-bit path running, so it is a depth-path test and not a
colorimetric claim: X'Y'Z' code values measured as if they were PQ are not HDR10+ for
that film.
Not verified: SIMD (the per-pixel loops are scalar double).

